WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.336
&gt;&gt; I want you to stop for a second and

00:00:05.336 --> 00:00:08.746
think about when I say customer
experience for you, what does that mean?

00:00:08.746 --> 00:00:13.124
If you're anything like the students that
I normally see in class, what you think

00:00:13.124 --> 00:00:17.312
about when customer experience is
literally the time in which the customer's

00:00:17.312 --> 00:00:21.133
interact with whatever service person
you have in your organization.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:24.037
That's the kind of answer that I typically
get when people think about customer

00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:24.569
experience.

00:00:24.569 --> 00:00:28.562
But then what I do is I ask people,
talk to me about your last vacation and

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:32.111
I want you to spend a minute and
think about your last vacation.

00:00:32.111 --> 00:00:33.325
Describe to yourself and

00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:36.281
sort of recreate in your mind
what that experience was like.

00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:40.424
But when I ask them about their
experience of going on vacation,

00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:45.565
they talk about planning the vacation,
anticipating the vacation, what they

00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:50.647
do when they go on vacation and then
the memories that they have from vacation.

00:00:50.647 --> 00:00:55.430
The customer experience is not simply the
very narrow interaction with your product.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.784
It also includes a before and an after.

00:00:58.784 --> 00:01:03.376
And those before and afters are critical
to how people understand your product, and

00:01:03.376 --> 00:01:07.269
for your likelihood of achieving
the holy grail which is word of mouth.

00:01:07.269 --> 00:01:10.193
So in psychology,
we talked about three sources of utility.

00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:12.688
Utility is kind of like a measure
of psychological well-being.

00:01:12.688 --> 00:01:15.015
More utility is something better.

00:01:15.015 --> 00:01:18.219
Anticipated utility,
how much utility will I get?

00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:20.302
Okay, I think about buying a car.

00:01:20.302 --> 00:01:23.255
My experience with the car is
not just driving the car, but

00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:25.493
it's also thinking about which car to buy.

00:01:25.493 --> 00:01:27.539
Getting excited about that car.

00:01:27.539 --> 00:01:30.520
Experienced utility, how much utility or

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:36.080
how much well being am I experiencing from
the actual use of the product itself?

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.826
Okay and this is typically when people
think about a customer experience,

00:01:39.826 --> 00:01:42.747
they really focus on
the experience utility components.

00:01:42.747 --> 00:01:47.369
Retrospective utility,
what do I remember this to be like?

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:49.062
And the memory is extremely important,

00:01:49.062 --> 00:01:51.868
because the memory is what's going to
determine your word of mouth.

00:01:51.868 --> 00:01:54.709
All three of those need to be
managed to create a really

00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:56.739
truly winning customer experience.

00:01:56.739 --> 00:01:58.799
Experiences aren't flat, right?

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:01.667
You don't sort of experience
the same thing at all times.

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:02.807
You have a moment of delight.

00:02:02.807 --> 00:02:03.959
You have a moment of despair.

00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:05.745
You have a moment of meh.

00:02:05.745 --> 00:02:09.579
The way memory works is
in terms of snapshots.

00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:12.912
I remember moments in that experience.

00:02:12.912 --> 00:02:17.613
And why is that so critical, because it
tells you that what you need to manage is

00:02:17.613 --> 00:02:20.691
not the film, but
it's specific kind of moments.

00:02:20.691 --> 00:02:25.488
&gt;&gt; Understand how consumers experience and
rewire your approach to product strategy.

00:02:25.488 --> 00:02:28.010
Learn more about
Innovative Product Leadership,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.894
The Emerging Chief Product Officer.

00:02:29.894 --> 00:02:39.894
